          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Jane\",\"addressee\":\"St John\",\"anchor_quote\":\"There is no dishonour, no breach of promise, no desertion in the case.\\nI am not under the slightest obligation to go to India, especially with\\nstrangers. With you I would have ventured much, because I admire,\\nconfide in, and, as a sister, I love you; but I am convinced that, go\\nwhen and with whom I would, I should not live long in that climate.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.98,\"reason\":\"Jane explicitly avows love for St John. The qualification “as a sister” identifies it as familial or sororal affection rather than romantic love, but does not diminish its clear truth-presenting force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"because I admire,\\nconfide in, and, as a sister, I love you\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.97,\"reason\":\"The focal avowal does not itself promise future conduct or undertake an obligation. Jane expressly denies being bound, and her separate offer to accompany St John only as an assistant must not be transferred onto “I love you.”\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I am not under the slightest obligation to go to India, especially with\\nstrangers.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"There is no dishonour, no breach of promise, no desertion in the case.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"The expression occurs within a deliberate explanation of Jane’s reasons and relationship to St John, not as a reflexive or affective verbal outburst. It is emotionally meaningful, but its force remains an avowal.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"With you I would have ventured much, because I admire,\\nconfide in, and, as a sister, I love you\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.92,\"reason\":\"No substantial residual force is needed beyond T. The sororal qualification helps define the kind of love avowed, while the surrounding refusal and explanation do not become part of the focal expression’s core force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"as a sister, I love you\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Jane is disputing St John’s claim that she has promised or undertaken to go to India. She explains that she would have ventured much specifically with him because she admires, trusts, and loves him as a sister, while maintaining that she is under no obligation and believes the climate would kill her. The avowal distinguishes familial affection from marital or romantic consent.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The focal expression undertakes nothing. In the surrounding exchange Jane separately offers to accompany St John as his assistant rather than his wife, but he rejects that arrangement and proposes referral to another missionary. Jane then expressly denies any promise or obligation to go to India. Thus no undertaking should be attributed to “I love you,” and the neighbouring proposal has no mutual uptake.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied passages are recognisable as Charlotte Brontë’s Jane Eyre.\",\"limitations\":\"The evidence clearly establishes the represented sororal avowal and the absence of undertaking force in the focal words. It does not by itself warrant broader conclusions about every aspect of Jane’s relationship with St John beyond the supplied passages.\"}"
        }
